+# Background Processes
+
+Run long-lived commands (dev servers, test watchers, local APIs, builds) in the
+background without blocking the conversation, and let them wake the agent.
+
+```bash
+pi -e packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/background-processes/index.ts
+```
+
+## Tools
+
+| Tool | Parameters | Returns |
+|---|---|---|
+| `bg_run` | `{ command, cwd?, name?, wake?: { ready?, error? }, cleanup?: { command, timeoutSec? } }` | process record |
+| `bg_logs` | `{ id, offset?, limit? }` | `{ id, offset, lines, total }`; a page is at most 64 KB, newest lines by default |
+| `bg_list` | `{}` | `{ processes }` |
+| `bg_kill` | `{ id, timeoutSec? }` | final record plus `cleanup: { ran, exitCode, failed } \| null` |
+| `bg_watch` | `{ id, ready?, error?, exit? }` | record; `null` removes a pattern, `exit: false` silences the exit wake |
+
+A process record has `id`, `name`, `command`, `cwd`, `pid`, `state`
+(`running` / `exited` / `killed`), `exitCode`, `startedAt`, `endedAt`
+(ISO-8601 strings), and `logLines`. Every tool returns its object as JSON text
+and as the result `details`.
+
+## Waking the agent
+
+A wake is a custom message with `customType: "background-process"` and
+`details: { id, name, reasons, exitCode, matchedLine }`.
+
+- Exit always wakes (once); `ready` and `error` wake only when a pattern was
+  given. `ready` fires at most once per process, `error` at most once per
+  delivery window.
+- While the agent is streaming, the wake is delivered after the current tool
+  batch, before the next model call. While idle, it starts a new turn.
+- Reasons for the same process between two deliveries collapse into one
+  message; several processes produce one message each, in first-fire order.
+- Nothing wakes after the exit wake, after `bg_kill`, or after `bg_watch`
+  silenced the reason.
+- The wake goes to the session that started the process while it is active,
+  otherwise to the current active session.
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+Processes belong to the pi process. `/reload`, `/new`, `/fork`, and tree
+navigation keep them running; any session in the same pi process can list,
+read, and kill them. When pi exits (normally or on `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`) every
+managed process group is stopped with `SIGTERM` then `SIGKILL`, including
+children the command spawned, and pi still terminates.
+
+Logs stream to `<sessionDir>/background-processes/<pi-pid>/<id>.log` with the
+final record beside them, so a later pi process that resumes the session can
+`bg_list` finished records and page their logs.
